1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,952
Content Localization lets one page exist in several languages, with each translation stored

2
00:00:06,952 --> 00:00:09,100
as a content item of its own.

3
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:13,764
In this video, you add the Localization part to a content type, create

4
00:00:13,764 --> 00:00:17,630
translations, manage them, and control who can translate.

5
00:00:22,230 --> 00:00:26,212
A multilingual site needs the same page in several languages.

6
00:00:26,212 --> 00:00:31,560
With the Content Localization module, each language is a separate content item, with

7
00:00:31,560 --> 00:00:34,710
its own content and its own publication status.

8
00:00:35,460 --> 00:00:38,708
The Localization part links the items together.

9
00:00:38,708 --> 00:00:40,363
It stores two values:

10
00:00:40,363 --> 00:00:44,837
the culture of the item, such as French, and a localization set, an

11
00:00:44,837 --> 00:00:48,760
identifier that every translation of the same item shares.

12
00:00:49,510 --> 00:00:53,716
An item created with the part gets the default culture of the site.

13
00:00:53,716 --> 00:00:58,787
Creating a translation copies the item into another supported culture, as a draft.

14
00:00:58,787 --> 00:01:01,610
You then translate the copy and publish it.

15
00:01:05,861 --> 00:01:09,800
Content Localization is a feature, so you turn it on first.

16
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:13,678
Open Tools, Features, and search for Content Localization.

17
00:01:13,678 --> 00:01:16,951
It appears in the Internationalization category.

18
00:01:17,405 --> 00:01:21,733
The module builds on the Localization feature, which is already enabled on this

19
00:01:21,733 --> 00:01:23,871
site and holds its list of cultures.

20
00:01:23,871 --> 00:01:27,485
Click Enable, and the page confirms that the feature was enabled.

21
00:01:27,945 --> 00:01:31,077
Next, open Settings, Localization, Cultures.

22
00:01:31,077 --> 00:01:36,402
This page lists the supported cultures by their codes, here for English, French

23
00:01:36,402 --> 00:01:39,911
and Spanish, and marks one as the Default culture.

24
00:01:39,911 --> 00:01:42,605
A separate video covers it in detail.

25
00:01:46,893 --> 00:01:51,178
A translation needs a content type that has the Localization part.

26
00:01:51,178 --> 00:01:56,473
Open Design, Content Definition, Content Types, and click Edit on the Article type.

27
00:01:56,926 --> 00:01:58,210
Click Add Parts.

28
00:01:58,210 --> 00:02:01,829
Each part you can attach is listed with its description.

29
00:02:01,829 --> 00:02:05,156
Select Localization, then click Add Selected Parts.

30
00:02:05,644 --> 00:02:11,299
Localization appears in the Parts list of the Article type, with a message confirming the addition.

31
00:02:11,299 --> 00:02:13,561
The part has no settings of its own.

32
00:02:13,561 --> 00:02:18,194
Items that already exist receive their culture when you save them in the editor.

33
00:02:22,378 --> 00:02:27,440
The Article type already has an item, About, created before the part was added.

34
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:31,132
Open Content, Content Items, and list the Article items.

35
00:02:31,132 --> 00:02:35,718
The About row shows neither a culture badge nor a Localizations button.

36
00:02:36,174 --> 00:02:39,549
Click Edit, then scroll to the bottom of the editor.

37
00:02:39,549 --> 00:02:42,575
A collapsed section shows the Current Culture.

38
00:02:42,575 --> 00:02:47,114
Open it, and it asks you to save the item before creating localizations.

39
00:02:47,579 --> 00:02:48,716
Click Publish.

40
00:02:48,716 --> 00:02:52,410
The About article receives the default culture of the site.

41
00:02:52,410 --> 00:02:57,639
Back in the list, a globe badge shows the culture, and a Localizations button appears.

42
00:03:01,890 --> 00:03:05,929
Click Edit on About again and open the Current Culture section.

43
00:03:05,929 --> 00:03:10,671
It lists the other supported cultures, French and Spanish, each with a plus

44
00:03:10,671 --> 00:03:13,070
icon because no translation exists.

45
00:03:13,533 --> 00:03:15,053
Click French (France).

46
00:03:15,053 --> 00:03:19,178
The module copies the article into that culture as a draft, then opens

47
00:03:19,178 --> 00:03:23,683
the copy in the editor with a message that the localized version was created.

48
00:03:24,149 --> 00:03:28,315
The copy keeps the English title and subtitle, ready to translate.

49
00:03:28,315 --> 00:03:32,482
Its permalink is empty, because the module clears it so that it is

50
00:03:32,482 --> 00:03:35,259
generated again from the translated title.

51
00:03:35,720 --> 00:03:38,061
Type the French title and subtitle.

52
00:03:38,061 --> 00:03:42,914
At the bottom, the Current Culture section shows French (France) for this item,

53
00:03:42,914 --> 00:03:46,740
and links to the English item and to the missing Spanish one.

54
00:03:47,193 --> 00:03:48,301
Click Publish.

55
00:03:48,301 --> 00:03:52,071
A translation is a content item of its own, so it is published

56
00:03:52,071 --> 00:03:57,393
separately from the English article, and its permalink is generated from the French title.

57
00:04:01,585 --> 00:04:06,186
The Localizations button on a list row shows every other culture.

58
00:04:06,186 --> 00:04:09,750
French (France) has a pen icon because it exists.

59
00:04:09,750 --> 00:04:14,805
Spanish (Spain) has a plus icon, so click it to create that translation.

60
00:04:15,258 --> 00:04:20,529
Enter the Spanish title and subtitle, then click Save Draft instead of Publish.

61
00:04:20,529 --> 00:04:24,808
The Spanish translation stays unpublished until you publish it.

62
00:04:25,272 --> 00:04:28,681
Back in the list, each translation is a row of its own.

63
00:04:28,681 --> 00:04:33,095
The globe badge shows the culture, and the Published or Draft badge shows

64
00:04:33,095 --> 00:04:34,772
the status of each item.

65
00:04:35,228 --> 00:04:39,650
To see one language, open the Culture drop-down and choose a culture.

66
00:04:39,650 --> 00:04:44,898
The list shows only the items in that culture, and All cultures removes the filter.

67
00:04:49,091 --> 00:04:53,609
On the site, each published translation is served at its own address.

68
00:04:53,609 --> 00:04:58,911
Click View on the English article to open it, with its English title and subtitle.

69
00:04:59,369 --> 00:05:04,052
The French translation opens at a different address, generated from its own title,

70
00:05:04,052 --> 00:05:06,499
and shows the French title and subtitle.

71
00:05:06,956 --> 00:05:10,220
A visitor reaches a translation through its address.

72
00:05:10,220 --> 00:05:15,173
Letting visitors switch between languages is the job of the Content Culture Picker

73
00:05:15,173 --> 00:05:17,706
feature, which a separate video covers.

74
00:05:21,902 --> 00:05:24,547
Two permissions control translation.

75
00:05:24,547 --> 00:05:30,216
Under Access Control, Roles, click Edit on the Author role, and search for Localize.

76
00:05:30,216 --> 00:05:33,492
They appear in the Content Localization group.

77
00:05:33,942 --> 00:05:38,162
Localize content for others lets a role translate any item.

78
00:05:38,162 --> 00:05:43,745
Localize own content limits translation to items the user created, and the first

79
00:05:43,745 --> 00:05:46,082
permission implies the second.

80
00:05:46,538 --> 00:05:51,304
By default, the Author and Contributor roles hold only Localize own content, and

81
00:05:51,304 --> 00:05:54,130
the Administrator and Editor roles hold both.

82
00:05:54,130 --> 00:05:57,678
A user also needs permission to edit content of that type.

83
00:05:59,678 --> 00:06:03,515
Each translation is a content item of its own, tied to the others

84
00:06:03,515 --> 00:06:05,191
by the Localization part.

85
00:06:05,191 --> 00:06:09,515
Enable the module, add the part to a content type, and create translations

86
00:06:09,515 --> 00:06:11,352
from the editor or the list.

87
00:06:11,352 --> 00:06:12,758
Thanks for watching.

